{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c3ad","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0ca08-6c63-7352-b762-5bc242c771c8"},"sequence":49,"recorded_at":1780531400000093,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"cd8feb9b-6d69-49ce-a85c-818ca818e251","payload_type":"task.lifecycle.output","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"task_lifecycle","command_id":"cd8feb9b-6d69-49ce-a85c-818ca818e251","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"cd8feb9b-6d69-49ce-a85c-818ca818e251"},"task_stream":{"kind":"task","id":"01a0ca08-8d05-7ca1-9211-46627462a990"},"task_id":"01a0ca08-8d05-7ca1-9211-46627462a990","event":{"kind":"output","task_id":"01a0ca08-8d05-7ca1-9211-46627462a990","chunk":"{\n  \"chunk_id\": \"exec-2-1\",\n  \"command\": \"cat /Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/agent-session-manager/.temp/resources/TASK-260830-1c28dz/TASK-260830-1c28dz_rework-rev10.md\",\n  \"description\": \"Read latest rework instructions rev10\",\n  \"exit_code\": 0,\n  \"terminal_status\": \"completed\",\n  \"output\": \"THIS BRIEF SUPERSEDES TASK-260830-1c28dz_rework-rev9.md and every earlier precondition. Do exactly what is written here.\\nRework run for TASK-260830-1c28dz. Revision 9 was reviewed by RUN-260922-2ec7ab (codex gpt-6-astra low) and routed CHANGES REQUESTED: **one P1**, two P2. Read TASK-260830-1c28dz_review-verdict-rev9.md first.\\n\\n1. **P1 — the peer census still turns invalid identity evidence into \\\"no peers\\\", and this is the third round of the same invariant.** Revision 8 read a directory at the receipt path as absence; you fixed the directory arm and the reviewer moved one arm over. Now `internal/termbind/overlap.go:65-72` decodes a receipt and checks it only against the requested session and instance — **it never checks the decoded client ID against the `<client UUID>.json` filename it was read from** — and then excludes any receipt whose *content* names the requesting client. So a valid receipt stored at another client's path vanishes from the census instead of refusing a corrupt namespace. `TestReviewAttachPeerFilenameMismatchFailsClosed` attaches B, moves B's valid receipt bytes to A's filename, then drives B again with `multi_attach` absent: success, a newly persisted B receipt, `InputAuthorized:true`, writable vector. B no longer has a receipt at its own key, so this is not a validated durable replay. The landed `AttachStore.Lookup` **already performs exactly the client-pair comparison you are missing**, at its keyed read boundary — use it rather than re-deriving the rule.\\n   **Stop enumerating bad shapes. Invert the rule.** The census must admit an entry only when it can positively parse it as a valid peer receipt whose client identity matches its own durable key, and refuse everything else in the receipt namespace as corruption before any receipt or vector is created. A blacklist of known-bad shapes (directory, now filename mismatch) will keep losing to the next shape; a whitelist cannot. Retain the session and instance checks and the staging/non-receipt rules, drive the negative through `Execute` asserting **no receipt, no vector, no input authorization**, and ship an admitting narrowing witness. B44 defers liveness; it does not authorise treating invalid identity evidence as an empty census.\\n2. **P2-A — you made the escalation success path unreachable.** Revision 9 correctly adds context cancellation to the read-only probes, but `backend.go:419` and `:469` use the **same `runner.deadline`** for the initial graceful wait and for the fresh confirmation after `kill-session`, and the graceful budget is already exhausted by the time escalation happens. The second `probeContext` is therefore cancelled immediately even though the operation deadline and authorization are still valid, so a cancellation-honouring executor can never observe the newly closed session. `TestReviewStopEscalationWithContextHonoringRunner` reuses your own committed escalation scenario and changes only one thing — the runner honours `ctx.Err()` before delegating, which is exactly `OSRunner`/`exec.CommandContext` semantics — and the calls end at `[send-keys has-session kill-session]` with the final `has-session` never given its chance. Distinguish the **graceful wait budget** from the **post-escalation observation budget**, which should draw on the operation deadline. Keep the fresh authorization, generation and operation-deadline rechecks before the kill, keep a timeout classified as unknown and never as closure, and add a context-honouring **success** witness for the escalation path beside the existing hung-probe refusal witnesses.\\n3. **P2-B — the importer outcome evidence regressed to the shape you had already fixed.** In revision 5 you delivered the right artefact and the reviewer verified it: a fixed corpus keyed on `(package, entry, input)` with outcomes as values, 193 shared keys, zero moved. Revision 9's results §7 labels both comparisons outcome-keyed, but the first one keys `(package, test)` top-level verdicts — `suite_grid.py` does exactly that — and the actual entry-outcome driver now imports only four packages, of which `scalar` is outside the reverse-importer closure. **33 of the 36 closure packages have no entry in the corpus**, and the 28-key corpus, though identical on base and candidate, cannot support a complete-set claim. `importer-audit.json` names every missing package. Restore the revision-5 artefact and extend it: stable `(package, production entry, input)` keys, actual returned decisions and effects, the same inputs on base and candidate, every moved admission or refusal class named, over the complete computed importer set. Keep suite verdicts as supplementary and state the finite corpus's limits honestly. Do not relabel a suite grid as an outcome grid.\\n\\nPROCESS. Keep the candidate UNCOMMITTED; exactly one leaf's worth of delta on top of the checkpoint; base `d4bd91d0`, and if trunk moves refresh through the managed path and repeat the path-by-path audit. Never commit on the Story branch, never edit `.task-board` directly, never self-accept, and do NOT touch `internal/traceability` — the final leaf g0pcnt carries the registry. The overlap liveness subsystem remains TASK-260922-vcx6yo's; this leaf owes only the fail-closed refusal and the recorded bound. Keep the zero-real-tmux determinism property. Run your own package tests, the full importer closure, `GOOS=windows GOARCH=amd64 go vet ./...`, and the harness BEFORE handoff, then the full configured suite once from `spawn.worktree_isolation.validation.commands`. Update the results with a finding-by-finding table and the restored outcome corpus; update the conformance matrix, attach the **current** evidence archive and the standalone results, verify every attachment by reading it back off the board and comparing digests, then `task-board handoff TASK-260830-1c28dz --role developer` so revision 10 is constructed, and exit promptly. Hand off no later than 3 hours in. Canonical CLI /Users/iv/.curator/global/bin/task-board. Model: muse-spark max. The independent reviewer will be codex gpt-6-astra low.\\nREJECTION PATTERNS THE INDEPENDENT REVIEWER HAS ALREADY CAUGHT ON THIS EPIC (2026-09-17: TASK-260830-1geqhj rev1, TASK-260830-24z2b3 rev2, TASK-260830-219okr rev3 were all routed CHANGES REQUESTED, and between them these six shapes account for every finding). Read this as a checklist you run against your own work BEFORE handoff; each item names what a reviewer will do to your candidate.\\n(a) Circular expectations. An assertion that compares a production value against a production constant (`got.Code == pkg.SomeUnsupportedCode`) pins nothing: swapping the constant to another registered value with the same exit keeps the suite green. Assert the LITERAL token from the pinned specification text, at the production entry, and make sure the literal actually occurs in a committed test — not only in a comment. Derive every expectation from the spec, never from the implementation.\\n(b) A rule pinned at the helper instead of the entry. A test that calls the inner helper directly proves the helper; it says nothing about whether the composed production entry still reaches it. Drive every rule through the entry point the conformance matrix names as its call site, and if a rule is implemented TWICE (a Build path and a Decode path, an exported entry and its internal sibling), each side needs its own narrowing mutant — a rule pinned only on one side leaves the other side unmeasured, and reviewers plant exactly there.\\n(c) Forking a landed gate. Never re-implement what internal/environ, internal/canonicaljson, internal/scalar or another landed owner already gates (string bounds in CHARACTERS not bytes, uint53, digests, UUIDv7, timestamps, sorted-unique arrays, closed-shape decoding, the AX number model that forbids floating point, values at or beyond 2^53 and rounding-and-continuing). Delegate to it and keep only your package-specific rules. Every fork this epic produced became a defect: a byte-measured bound, an extension value rounded at 2^60, nested duplicate members collapsed by a lenient decode.\\n(d) A census or an inventory standing in for behaviour. A reflection census of field NAMES does not prove that a cached observation cannot authorize; an arm inventory keyed by message text does not prove a condition edit is caught. Drive the input, assert the effect.\\n(e) Mutant rows that do not measure what they claim. An arm-delete (`&& false`) is not a narrowing; a row patching two sites whose kill comes from one is one measured site reported as two; a plant with no raw log and no subprocess exit is a verdict line, not evidence. Ship one raw log per plant per run, state narrowing vs arm-delete per row, and include an applied harmless SURVIVED control so the harness is proven able to report a survivor.\\n(f) Prose that outruns the tests. \\\"Composes X\\\", \\\"never admits what decoding refuses\\\", \\\"nothing re-implemented\\\", \\\"no cached sentinel can authorize\\\" are censusable claims: a reviewer greps for the callers and plants the counter-example. Before you write a sentence like that in a doc comment, README, LOGBOOK or TRACEABILITY row, make a test fail without the property. If you cannot, state it as an explicit BOUND instead, naming what is not covered.\\nFinally: report the coverage ratio you MEASURED, not the one you planned. Two of the three rejected revisions claimed a complete ratio and the reviewer measured a smaller one; an honest smaller number with named gaps is accepted, an inflated one is a finding.\\n\",\n  \"original_output_bytes\": 9523,\n  \"original_output_tokens\": 2381,\n  \"truncated\": false\n}","final_result":true}}}
